<section class="reference-section" id="shvetaketu-uddalaka"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Shvetaketu and Uddalaka · Tat Tvam Asi</h2><a href="/shvetaketu-uddalaka.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Chandogya Upanishad · Chapter 6 · Gita Press, Gorakhpur.</strong> Owner-supplied Sanskrit text with Shankara-bhashya and Hindi explanation. This is an original condensed English reader, not a translation. The private scan is not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>6.1 · PDF pp. 577–585</strong><small>Shvetaketu returns after twelve years of study; Uddalaka asks about the instruction by which the unknown becomes understood and gives the clay and metal examples.</small></li><li><strong>6.2–4 · PDF pp. 586–626</strong><small>Sat as “one without a second,” the rejection of arising from non-being, the unfolding through heat, water and food, and the relation of names and forms to their material ground.</small></li><li><strong>6.5–7 · PDF pp. 627–643</strong><small>Food, water and heat in the person; the fifteen-day fast and the return of memory after nourishment.</small></li><li><strong>6.8 · PDF pp. 644–666</strong><small>Deep sleep, the tethered bird, tracing hunger and thirst to their roots, and the first occurrence of the repeated refrain.</small></li><li><strong>6.9–11 · PDF pp. 667–679</strong><small>Honey gathered from many flowers, rivers entering the ocean and life pervading the tree.</small></li><li><strong>6.12–13 · PDF pp. 680–688</strong><small>The subtle banyan seed and salt dissolved throughout water.</small></li><li><strong>6.14–16 · PDF pp. 689–713</strong><small>The traveller from Gandhara, the dying person, the heated axe and the conclusion of the repeated teaching.</small></li></ol><p><strong>Editorial boundary.</strong> The page retains the sequence of examples and the repeated refrain rather than extracting <em>Tat Tvam Asi</em> from its dialogue. “Being” and “the Real” are accessible glosses for <em>Sat</em>; “That you are” is used as a compact English rendering, not as a replacement for commentary. Shankara’s Advaita interpretation belongs to this edition, while other Vedanta traditions understand the statement differently. The fasting episode is not presented as medical or devotional guidance. The reflection question is editorial.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated interpretations used once each. Uddalaka, Shvetaketu, the hermitage, clothing and objects are imagined. The clay, seed, salt and rivers visualize examples from the passage without personifying Brahman, Sat or Atman.</p></div></section>

